<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Imperialism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">Imperialism</a> characterises a period of colonial expansion by the major Western powers and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> during the late 19th and early 20th centuries. Its main participants were the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, which conquered almost all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. It reflected rivalries among the great powers, the desire for new resources and markets and a civilising mission ethos, sharpened by the Long Depression of 1873–1896; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a> of 1884–1885 set effective occupation as the test of a claim. Many of its colonies gained independence after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word new separates this period from earlier imperial activity, such as the ancient empires and the first wave of European colonization. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> and the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> in the 1820s ended that first era, and especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> they exposed the weaknesses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercantilism">mercantilism</a>. Between 1815 and 1871 Britain, the workshop of the world, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s dominant military and economic power. By the end of the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> challenged it: in 1870 Britain held 31.8 percent of the world's manufacturing capacity, the United States 23.3 percent and Germany 13.2 percent, and by 1910 the shares were 14.7, 35.3 and 15.9 percent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The British East India Company annexed most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in 1757 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1849, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> then expanded into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. France annexed all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> in the 1880s and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> in the following decade, leaving Siam as a neutral buffer. Between 1850 and 1914 Britain brought nearly 30 percent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>'s population under its control, France 15 percent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> 9 percent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> 7 percent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> 1 percent. Only Liberia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> escaped European control by 1914.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a> of 1884–1885 defined effective occupation as the test for recognising a claim in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and none of the fifteen nations present was African. Direct rule required routine armed force, and uprisings were put down harshly, most brutally in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herero-wars">Herero Wars</a> of 1904 to 1907 and the Maji Maji Rebellion of 1905 to 1907.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Motives included a civilising mission, which many missionaries took up, and the Dutch Ethical Policy announced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmina-of-the-netherlands">Queen Wilhelmina</a> in 1901. John A. Hobson, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-kautsky">Karl Kautsky</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a> argued that surplus capital sought investment abroad. Critics note that this does not explain expansion by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, which had little surplus capital, or by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which were net borrowers, and that occupation often cost more than it returned. Rivalry added tension before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, as in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fashoda">Fashoda</a> incident of 1898, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a> of 1898 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> of 1904–1905 marked the rise of the United States and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Many colonies gained independence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a> that followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “New Imperialism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Britain, the sociologist Benjamin Kidd used the notion in Social Evolution (1894) to account for what he called the Anglo-Saxon and Latin overflowing his boundaries, and it served to justify the <b>New Imperialism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/social-darwinism">Social Darwinism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
